                <pb facs="0356" n="268" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv02/pg268"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Paris Peace Conf. 871.0144/9: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bliss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Acting Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1919-01-14T18:00:00+00:00"
                            >January 14, 1919—6 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6738. Following joint telegram from Allied Ministers at Jassy, dated Army of the
                    Danube, January 10th: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The Roumanian Government and public opinion are extremely affected by
                            the attitude of the Allies in Dobroudja. It is observed that the
                            invasion of this part of the national territory the integrity of which
                            we have guaranteed is the result of Roumania’s intervention on our side
                            and the Entente should therefore have purely and simply handed this
                            territory over to the Roumanian Government. On the contrary the Allies
                            have requested Roumania not to make a military occupation in Dobroudja
                            and their local agents have even opposed the sending of Roumanian custom
                            officers giving as an excuse ‘the opposition of the Bulgarian
                            Government.’ This state of affairs is aggravated by the fact that the
                            Allied officers fraternize with the Bulgarians and give the impression
                            that they are granting preferential treatment to the enemy to the
                            detriment of its [<hi rendition="#italic">their?</hi>] Ally, Roumania,
                            concerning the affairs of the latter. The sovereigns themselves have
                            voiced to us their complaints on this attitude as well as their grave
                            apprehension as to the consequences that such attitude can lead to in
                            the extremely difficult situation in which the country finds
                            itself.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bliss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
